## 3. TypeScript & Types

### Path Aliases
USE the configured path aliases — NEVER use deep relative paths (`../../../`):

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
import { PrismaService } from '@src/common/db/prisma.service';
import { LoggerService } from 'market-logger/logger';

// ❌ BAD
import { PrismaService } from '../../../common/db/prisma.service';
```

Aliases defined in `tsconfig.json`:
- `@src/*` → `./src/*`
- `market-logger/logger` → `libs/logger/src`

### Type Definitions
ALWAYS use `interface` for object shapes (ESLint enforces `consistent-type-definitions: interface`):

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
interface ICreateStrategyDto { name: string; description?: string; }

// ❌ BAD
type CreateStrategyDto = { name: string; };
```

NEVER use `any`. Use `unknown`, specific types, or `Prisma.*` generated types:

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
const updateData: Prisma.ApiKeyUpdateInput = {};

// ❌ BAD
const updateData: any = {};
```

### Type-Only Imports
ALWAYS use `import type` for type-only imports (ESLint enforces this):

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
import type { Request } from 'express';
import { Prisma } from '@prisma/client';

// ❌ BAD
import { Request } from 'express';
```

### Other Rules
- ALWAYS use `const` (never `var`, avoid `let` when value doesn't change)
- NEVER use `await` inside loops — use `Promise.all` or `$transaction` instead
- NEVER use default exports (`import/no-default-export` is enforced)
- Unused variables prefixed with `_` are allowed: `_unusedParam`

